    {
     "type": "video",
     "title": "Video: what is the accusative?",
     "videoId": "fPKH95g_9cs",
     "channel": "YourGermanTeacher (Luzi)",
     "start": 25,
     "end": 713,
     "note": "Chapters: 0:25 what is the accusative · 4:04 the articles · 5:28 examples · 9:23 word order · 11:22 question of the day."
    },
    {
     "type": "note",
     "title": "Subject and object",
     "md": "**Nominativ** = the subject — who does something. **Akkusativ** = the direct object — what/who the action happens to.\n\n- {de:Das Kind ist glücklich.} → only a subject\n- {de:Das Kind sieht **einen Hund**.} → *Kind* = subject, *Hund* = object (Akkusativ)\n\nMost verbs take an accusative object — and **haben always does**: {de:Ich habe einen Bruder.} (lesson 11)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "title": "Only der-words change",
     "intro": "Tap a row to hear it.",
     "cols": [
      "",
      "Nominativ",
      "Akkusativ"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "der (m)",
       "der / ein Hund",
       "den / einen Hund"
      ],
      [
       "die (f)",
       "die / eine E-Mail",
       "die / eine E-Mail"
      ],
      [
       "das (n)",
       "das / ein Buch",
       "das / ein Buch"
      ],
      [
       "plural",
       "die Stifte",
       "die Stifte"
      ]
     ],
     "skipFirst": true,
     "note": "That's all: **der → den, ein → einen** (and *kein → keinen*). Everything else stays the same."
    },
    {
     "type": "phrases",
     "title": "Examples from the video",
